use /lib
from /scope/harness-integration import HarnessIntegrationScope
from /scope/project-directory import ProjectDirectoryScope
from /scope/lsp import LSP
from /scope/prompt-mode/message-list import MessageList
from /scope/settings-window import SettingsWindowScope
from /ui/components/history-list import HistoryListScope
from /ui/components/scroll-column import ScrollColumnScope

export scope ChatInputScope:
    concept: {ChatInputConcept}

concept ChatInputConcept:
    pitch:
        A multi-line, syntax-highlighted textbox that accepts plain text as well
        as snippets of Piton.

    shape: {ChatInputShape}

shape ChatInputShape:
    description:
        Input on the left, send button on the right.

        <Enter> allows for creating new lines, like Ctrl/Cmd+Enter sends it to
        the ${HarnessIntegrationScope}

        Sending is never blocked by the harness working: while it is, the send
        button on the Code, Chain, and Spec tabs reads "Queue" and sending adds
        the prompt to prompt mode's queue. On the Ask tab it always reads
        "Send", because a question is never queued. The button is only disabled
        while the input is empty.

    tabs:
        overview:
            The input and send button live inside the body of a tab bar, using
            gpui-kit's default tab styling. There are four tabs, in order: Code,
            Chain, Spec, and Ask. Code, Spec, and Ask are ordinary tabs. Chain
            is also a tab, just a small one, because it only shows an icon. All
            four sit flush against each other with no extra gaps, and no tab has
            more padding than a normal tab.

            Pressing <Tab> while in the chat input cycles from Code to Chain to
            Spec to Ask and back to Code, <Shift+Tab> the other way, and focus
            remains in the chat input.

        chain:
            Chain is a tab with unusual behaviour. It shows an icon rather than
            a label, reading "Code and Spec" as its tooltip, and selecting it
            selects both Code and Spec. It never shows a focus outline, and its
            icon looks evenly spaced between Code and Spec whichever of the two
            is selected.

            Unselected, it shows a broken chain dimmed to 50% opacity, and sits
            between Code and Spec like any other tab, overlapping neither of
            them.

            Selected, it shows a joined chain at full opacity. Selecting it
            animates as though it took a margin of -50% of its own width on both
            its left and right sides: Code and Spec slide together until they
            meet underneath it, so it ends up sitting on top of both.
            Deselecting it animates them apart again. While Chain is selected,
            Code and Spec both show as selected and read as a single unified
            tab, with no line between them. Chain itself has a transparent
            background and no left or right borders, so the joined Code and Spec
            show through underneath it.

        help:
            To the right of the tabs, filling the rest of the bar, a line of
            help text says what the selected tab is for, and changes as soon as
            another tab is selected. It is extra small and dimmer than muted
            text, set a little apart from the last tab, so it reads as a quiet
            note about the tab rather than as another tab. It is cut off rather
            than wrapped when the bar is too narrow.

            code:
                Changes the code, leaving the spec as it is.

            chain:
                Changes the code and the spec together.

            spec:
                Changes the spec, leaving the code as it is.

            ask:
                Asks a question about the code and the spec, changing neither.

        colour:
            The selected tab and the tab body holding the input are tinted: a
            subtle red for Code, a subtle blue for Spec, and a subtle green for
            Ask. With Chain selected, Code, Spec, and the body are all purple.
            The colour transitions smoothly whenever the selected tab changes,
            straight from the old tab's colour to the new one's. The tabs wrap
            around, so pressing <Tab> on Ask fades green straight to Code's red,
            and <Shift+Tab> on Code fades red straight to Ask's green; neither
            lights up Chain or Spec on the way.

    systemPrompt:
        The selected tab is the mode a prompt is sent in, and each mode gives
        the hidden anchor a systemPrompt property alongside its userPrompt. The
        code location is `codeRoot` and the spec location is `root`, as set in
        the project's piton.config.pi.

        code:
            We're working on the code located in <code location>. Don't edit the
            spec located in <spec location>.

        spec:
            We're working on the spec located in <spec location>. Don't edit the
            code located in <code location>.

        both:
            We're working on both the code located in <code location> and the
            spec located in <spec location>. Edit both of them.

        ask:
            We're only asking a question about the code located in <code
            location> and the spec located in <spec location>. Don't edit either
            of them.

        These are the defaults; each is saved with the project and can be edited
        in the @{SettingsWindowScope}.

        A prompt keeps the mode it was sent in while it waits in the queue.

    ask:
        Ask is for simply asking a question. A question runs straight away,
        separately from any Code, Chain, or Spec task, even while the harness is
        working on one. Any number of questions can be asked, and they all run
        at the same time; asking another never stops or waits for one already
        running.

        Each question's progress and results are shown in a task table, as in
        the @{MessageList}. The questions stack directly above the tabs, the
        newest nearest them, in a column that pushes the message list up to make
        room rather than covering it, so none of a running task's progress is
        ever hidden behind them. Each slides up out of the chat input with an
        animation as it is asked, the message list giving way above it. While
        the harness works on a question, it is a single row showing the latest
        thing the harness did. A finished question is a row with its status and
        first line.

        Once a question is complete, it opens onto a heading, with its status
        and first line, above its full task table, in the answer drawer, which
        it fills, scrolling once it is taller, with a @{ScrollColumnScope} and
        its lock to the bottom. Only one question is open at a time: opening
        another closes the one open back down to its row. An open question
        leaves the stack for the drawer. Clicking an open question's heading
        closes it down to its row in the stack, and clicking a finished
        question's row opens it again. Each question has a close button, which
        removes it and stops it if it is still running, leaving the others
        running. A closed question becomes a previous answer.

    answerSteps:
        For a question, only the answer matters, so the steps leading up to it
        are collapsed by default wherever a finished question's task table
        is shown: an open question, and a previous answer. The steps are every
        row up to and including the harness's last tool call; the answer is the
        rows after it. In their place, the table's first row reads "Show 1 step"
        or "Show N steps", muted, beside a right chevron, with the answer
        beneath it. A single click on that row expands the steps in place, above
        the answer, and the row then reads "Hide 1 step" or "Hide N steps"
        beside a down chevron; clicking it again collapses them. Each question's
        table is expanded or collapsed on its own, and a question closed into
        the previous answers starts collapsed again.

        A reply with no tool calls, or with nothing after its last one, has
        nothing to collapse and shows whole. A question still running shows its
        latest step as usual. Tasks are never collapsed like this, since their
        whole chain is of interest: see the @{MessageList}.

    previousAnswers:
        Previous answers are a @{HistoryListScope}, shown only while the Ask tab
        is selected, at the top of the questions stacked above the tabs. It
        lists every question asked before and closed: those saved with the
        project and those closed since. Its row reads "No previous answers",
        "1 previous answer", or "N previous answers", and while expanded,
        "Back to the questions". Expanded, the row and the list go into the
        answer drawer, the list sliding up out of the chat input. The questions
        asked from now on carry on the conversation the latest of them left off.

    answerDrawer:
        Only a question open onto its task table, or the previous answers
        expanded, are a drawer. Unlike the stack of question rows, which pushes
        what is above it, the drawer slides up out of the chat input over
        everything above it, the message list, any file beside it, and the
        stack, covering them rather than pushing them, and dims them: see the
        @{MessageList}. Closing it slides nothing back; the stack and the
        message list are where they were beneath it.

        The drawer opens to 80% of the height of the space above the chat input
        by default: the open question's table, or the previous answers list,
        grows to fill whatever the drawer's other rows leave, rather than
        stopping at a fixed height. It slides up out of the chat input as it
        opens.

        While open, the drawer is resizable by dragging its top edge, which
        shows a resize cursor and a thin highlight when hovered. It follows the
        pointer exactly, with no slide, and can be dragged from 20% to 95% of
        that space. The size it is dragged to is kept for as long as the
        application runs, for every question or list opened afterwards, rather
        than going back to 80%.

    selectingAnswerText:
        The text of an answer being viewed, in the answer drawer, whether an
        open question or a previous answer, can be selected by dragging across
        it, as any text can. When a drag that selects text ends within the
        drawer, a small popover appears just below and to the right of where the
        pointer was let go, floating above everything, with two small buttons
        side by side: "Copy", with a copy icon, and "Attach to prompt", with a
        paperclip icon.

        Copy puts the selected text on the clipboard. Attach to prompt adds it
        to the prompt as an attachment. Either one closes the popover and clears
        the selection. The popover also closes without doing anything when the
        mouse is pressed anywhere outside it, including to start selecting
        something else, or when the drawer closes.

    attachments:
        What is attached to the prompt is listed above the text input, between
        the tabs and the input, one row per attachment in the order they were
        attached, and only while there is anything attached. An attached piece
        of text shows a quote icon, the text's first line cut off with an
        ellipsis, and, when it runs to more than one line, how many lines it
        has, muted, and at the right end a small button with a cross, whose
        tooltip says it removes the attachment, which removes just that one.

        Attachments belong to the prompt being written, not to a tab: switching
        tabs keeps them all, and they go with the prompt whichever tab it is
        sent from. When the prompt is sent, its attachments are sent along with
        it and the list empties, as the text input does. A queued prompt keeps
        the attachments it was sent with.

    escape:
        Pressing <Escape> while in the chat input takes focus out of the chat
        input.

    growToFit:
        The text input will grow to fit the text inside of it, one row per
        visual line: both lines broken with <Enter> and long lines that soft
        wrap. It shrinks again as text is removed, and re-fits when the window
        is resized. It starts at a single row, lined up with the send button,
        and stops growing at 12 rows, after which it scrolls.

    autoFocus:
        The chat input should be auto-focused.

    syntaxHighlighting:
        Syntax highlighting will be supported via the [Tree Sitter
        Grammar](https://github.com/piton-lang/tree-sitter-piton)

    lsp: @{LSP}

    harnessMentions:
        overview:
            Typing `/` offers the harness's skills and commands, and typing `@`
            offers its agents, the way they are offered in the harness itself.
            The placeholder mentions both.

        trigger:
            A mention starts at `/` or `@` at the start of the input or after
            whitespace, so paths and email addresses never open it, and an at
            sign followed by an opening curly brace is still a Piton reference.
            Completions are requested after typing the `/` or `@`, and after
            each letter, digit, hyphen, underscore, or colon of the name that
            follows. Only inside a mention are a slash or a hyphen a trigger;
            outside of one they never reach the @{LSP}.

        offered:
            Skills and commands are those of the selected
            ${ProjectDirectoryScope}, of the user, and of the user's enabled
            plugins, a plugin's own being named `plugin:name`. Agents come from
            the same places, plus the harness's built-in agents. Each shows
            whether it is a skill, command, or agent, and its description as
            documentation when it has one. Every harness run reports the skills
            and agents it has, built-in ones included; the last report is saved
            with the project and offered too. Results are narrowed and ordered
            like Piton completions: matches at the start of a name before
            matches anywhere in it, then by name.

        accepting:
            Accepting a skill or command inserts `/name `, and accepting an
            agent inserts `@agent-name `, which the harness understands as a
            request to use that agent. They pass through the hidden anchor and
            its compilation unchanged, so a prompt starting with `/name` runs
            that skill or command. Mentions work without the @{LSP}.

    hiddenAnchor:
        While the user will be able to type plain text, behind the scenes,
        they'll be typing into a randomly-named piton anchor with a userPrompt
        property.  It's this compiled anchor that will be send to the
        @{HarnessIntegrationScope}

        attachedText:
            A prompt with attached text gives the hidden anchor an attachedText
            property: a list with an item per attached piece of text, in the
            order they were attached. Since attached text is taken as it is, not
            as Piton, each item is itself a list of the text's lines, each a
            quoted string, with quotes and backslashes escaped, so nothing in it
            is interpolated or evaluated. A prompt with nothing attached has no
            attachedText property.

            What the harness receives is the compiled userPrompt followed by the
            attached text: a line reading "Attached text:", then each piece as a
            fenced code block, whose fence is longer than any run of backticks
            in the text itself. This is also the compiled prompt shown in the
            task's header.
